WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.800
Hey, welcome back.

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:06.060
Two capacitors share charge, but they do not automatically share energy

00:00:06.060 --> 00:00:06.520
equally.

00:00:07.780 --> 00:00:08.500
Let's see why.

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:14.120
Start with a two microfarad capacitor at twelve volts and an uncharged four

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:15.040
microfarad capacitor.

00:00:16.220 --> 00:00:20.546
Disconnect any battery, then join corresponding plates so the capacitors end

00:00:20.546 --> 00:00:20.660
up

00:00:20.660 --> 00:00:21.180
in parallel.

00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:26.220
The joined positive node initially holds twenty four microcoulombs.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:29.820
After the real circuit settles, both capacitors have the same voltage.

00:00:30.680 --> 00:00:30.760
Why?

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:32.960
Their plates connect to the same two nodes.

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:38.180
Total capacitance is six microfarads, so final voltage is twenty four divided

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:40.340
by six, four volts.

00:00:40.340 --> 00:00:44.500
The two microfarad capacitor now holds eight microcoulombs and the four

00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:46.560
microfarad capacitor sixteen.

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:48.720
Their sum is still twenty four.

00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:49.900
Charge conservation checks.

00:00:50.480 --> 00:00:53.700
Now pause and predict: is stored energy also unchanged?

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:58.420
Initial energy is one half times two microfarads times twelve volts squared,

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:01.400
one hundred forty four microjoules.

00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:05.747
Final energy is one half times six microfarads times four volts squared, forty

00:01:05.747 --> 00:01:07.000
eight microjoules.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:10.500
Ninety six microjoules have left the electric field store.

00:01:11.260 --> 00:01:15.260
During a real transient, resistance and possibly radiation carry that energy

00:01:15.260 --> 00:01:15.560
away.

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:17.420
The charge has not vanished.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:22.740
An exactly zero resistance, zero inductance connection does not describe the

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:25.880
settling transient, so we assume eventual real equilibration.

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:31.456
For the general case, final voltage is C one V one plus C two V two, divided

00:01:31.456 --> 00:01:31.580
by

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:32.700
the sum of capacitances.

00:01:33.560 --> 00:01:35.940
Both initial voltages must use the same orientation.

00:01:36.620 --> 00:01:40.280
If the second capacitor is reversed, its voltage enters with a negative sign.

00:01:41.040 --> 00:01:44.140
Do not add positive charge magnitudes just because they look easier.

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:49.254
The released energy, for fixed capacitances and no battery, is one half times

00:01:49.254 --> 00:01:49.315
C

00:01:49.315 --> 00:01:53.380
one C two divided by their sum, times the initial voltage difference squared.

00:01:54.100 --> 00:01:57.780
It is nonnegative and becomes zero when the initial voltages already agree.

00:01:58.620 --> 00:02:01.820
Those two checks help catch sign mistakes before they become wrong answers.

00:02:02.112 --> 00:02:05.892
Now a phrase that can cause trouble: half filled with dielectric.

00:02:06.612 --> 00:02:07.072
Half of what?

00:02:07.672 --> 00:02:10.192
Draw the geometry before selecting a formula.

00:02:10.412 --> 00:02:13.917
First, the dielectric covers half the plate area and spans the full

00:02:13.917 --> 00:02:14.492
separation.

00:02:15.112 --> 00:02:16.752
The remaining area contains vacuum.

00:02:17.612 --> 00:02:21.092
Both regions run between the same two plates, so they have the same voltage.

00:02:21.312 --> 00:02:23.412
Their capacitances add in parallel.

00:02:31.452 --> 00:02:32.132
If empty capacitance is C zero and area fraction is f, the total is C zero

00:02:32.132 --> 00:02:32.472
times one minus f plus kappa times f.

00:02:32.472 --> 00:02:33.432
Kappa is the relative permittivity.

00:02:34.192 --> 00:02:37.952
With half the area filled and kappa three, the multiplier is one half plus

00:02:37.952 --> 00:02:39.192
three halves, two.

00:02:39.652 --> 00:02:42.932
An empty four microfarad capacitor becomes eight microfarads.

00:02:43.512 --> 00:02:47.472
At equal voltage, the dielectric region stores more free charge; do not force

00:02:47.472 --> 00:02:48.872
equal charges in the two regions.

00:02:49.532 --> 00:02:51.152
Now rotate the arrangement mentally.

00:02:51.632 --> 00:02:55.072
A slab covers the whole area, but only half the plate separation.

00:02:55.652 --> 00:02:57.352
The rest of the separation is vacuum.

00:02:57.932 --> 00:03:00.352
Each field line passes through both layers in succession.

00:03:01.132 --> 00:03:03.772
Voltage drops add, so these layers act in series.

00:03:04.612 --> 00:03:06.432
Assume no free charge at the internal interface.

00:03:07.052 --> 00:03:11.112
The normal electric displacement is continuous, and field in the dielectric is

00:03:11.112 --> 00:03:12.932
the vacuum field divided by kappa.

00:03:13.592 --> 00:03:17.598
The voltage is vacuum field times vacuum thickness, plus dielectric field

00:03:17.598 --> 00:03:17.872
times

00:03:17.872 --> 00:03:18.512
slab thickness.

00:03:19.212 --> 00:03:22.944
Dividing charge by that total voltage gives capacitance equal to C zero

00:03:22.944 --> 00:03:23.312
divided

00:03:23.312 --> 00:03:27.452
by one minus g plus g divided by kappa, where g is the slab thickness

00:03:27.452 --> 00:03:27.992
fraction.

00:03:28.792 --> 00:03:32.312
For g one half and kappa three, the multiplier is one point five.

00:03:32.812 --> 00:03:36.572
Our four microfarad capacitor becomes six microfarads, not eight.

00:03:37.192 --> 00:03:39.892
Same material, same word half, different result.

00:03:40.532 --> 00:03:45.212
Check the limits: zero filling gives C zero; complete filling gives kappa C

00:03:45.212 --> 00:03:47.972
zero; kappa one gives the empty value at every fraction.

00:03:48.612 --> 00:03:51.452
We assumed linear uniform dielectric and neglected fringing.

00:03:52.092 --> 00:03:54.432
Real boundary effects can need more detailed treatment.

00:03:55.092 --> 00:03:59.320
For JEE, the sketch tells you whether to add capacitances or add voltage

00:03:59.320 --> 00:03:59.672
drops.

00:03:59.976 --> 00:04:03.016
Let's finish with two shortcuts that need conditions.

00:04:03.956 --> 00:04:05.796
First, dielectric insertion and work.

00:04:06.696 --> 00:04:10.996
Start with two microfarads at ten volts and insert a dielectric of kappa three

00:04:10.996 --> 00:04:11.616
completely.

00:04:12.376 --> 00:04:13.336
Keep the battery connected.

00:04:14.296 --> 00:04:18.936
Voltage stays ten volts, capacitance becomes six microfarads and charge rises

00:04:18.936 --> 00:04:21.196
from twenty to sixty microcoulombs.

00:04:22.176 --> 00:04:25.976
Battery work is voltage times the charge increase: four hundred microjoules.

00:04:26.536 --> 00:04:30.736
Stored energy rises from one hundred to three hundred microjoules, a change of

00:04:30.736 --> 00:04:31.736
two hundred.

00:04:31.736 --> 00:04:32.596
Why are these different?

00:04:33.336 --> 00:04:37.636
During slow lossless insertion, the field pulls the material inward and an

00:04:37.636 --> 00:04:39.916
external agent can extract mechanical energy.

00:04:40.756 --> 00:04:45.056
Energy balance is change in stored energy equals battery work plus external

00:04:45.056 --> 00:04:45.396
work.

00:04:46.096 --> 00:04:48.556
External work is therefore minus two hundred microjoules.

00:04:49.236 --> 00:04:53.856
If we disconnect the battery first, charge stays twenty microcoulombs and no

00:04:53.856 --> 00:04:54.756
battery work occurs.

00:04:55.316 --> 00:04:59.816
Final stored energy is about thirty three point three three microjoules, so

00:04:59.816 --> 00:05:03.516
external work is about minus sixty six point six seven microjoules.

00:05:04.336 --> 00:05:07.876
These results assume quasistatic motion and negligible losses.

00:05:08.736 --> 00:05:10.876
State what is fixed and whose work is requested.

00:05:11.836 --> 00:05:15.376
Second, capacitors in a chain do not always have equal charge magnitudes.

00:05:16.136 --> 00:05:19.756
Picture two microfarads between a twelve volt terminal and a floating node B,

00:05:20.216 --> 00:05:23.336
then four microfarads between B and a zero volt terminal.

00:05:23.336 --> 00:05:27.476
The isolated middle node initially has plus six microcoulombs.

00:05:28.296 --> 00:05:32.536
Write the charge on each plate attached to B: two times B minus twelve, plus

00:05:32.536 --> 00:05:34.316
four times B, in microcoulombs.

00:05:34.856 --> 00:05:36.276
Their sum must equal six.

00:05:36.756 --> 00:05:40.756
So six B minus twenty four equals six, giving B five volts.

00:05:41.436 --> 00:05:45.376
The left capacitor has seven volts across it and charge magnitude fourteen

00:05:45.376 --> 00:05:45.936
microcoulombs.

00:05:46.556 --> 00:05:48.936
The right has five volts and twenty microcoulombs.

00:05:49.556 --> 00:05:53.636
On the middle node, signed charges are minus fourteen and plus twenty, summing

00:05:53.636 --> 00:05:55.016
to the specified plus six.

00:05:55.236 --> 00:05:59.256
If the node were neutral instead, B would be four volts and both charge

00:05:59.256 --> 00:06:01.036
magnitudes sixteen microcoulombs.

00:06:01.636 --> 00:06:04.436
The equal charge shortcut belongs to that neutral node case.

00:06:05.036 --> 00:06:09.236
Our routine is to draw connected nodes, preserve signed charge, and then check

00:06:09.236 --> 00:06:10.676
the energy transfers separately.
